--- MESSAGE ---
A call is f(a, b), positional then name:value, and the parameters/par call form retires

The call syntax agreed on 2026-09-13 lands: an application is written as every reader expects —
`f(a, b)`, positional arguments first, then `name:value`, each parameter named at most once, an omitted
one taking its declared default. A call is ONE token on the drake surface (brackets glue, whitespace
inside them never ends a token), so its parentheses bound it and every device that existed to bound an
argument list that could not bound itself goes: `<fn> parameters` with its `par` lines, `par = <name>`,
the `.member parameters` chain continuations, and the `[ ]` an application wore as an argument or a
`++` operand. `parameters` and `par` remain the declaration words. `=` leaves the reserved set; no
bare `=` is left on the surface.

The parser trees the call by splitting the glued token at its final depth-0 `( )` group, the arguments
at depth-0 commas, and the head at depth-0 dots — the last call element closing the receiver, which
rebuilds the nested spine the chain lines used to spell one call per line. Inside the parentheses the
reserved words are ordinary text (`LazyList.from(start, step)`); only `++` is read there. A head-less
group with a depth-0 comma is a tuple; `(x)` is an opaque leaf. The emitter writes every value on one
line. A `()` call in a String-typed slot renders unquoted in the engine, as `++` already did.

Thirteen corpus files convert (eight in src/main, two mods actors, three scenario files); their JSON
and Scala are unchanged. DrakeParseTest's structural tests move to the call and gain the at-most-once
rule (606 tests). Because every parenthesised call now trees, the ~105 host-opaque call strings still
in the JSON corpus come back as trees on parse, so the report-only surface-loss headline rises until
the JSON is re-canonicalized from the drake — the next increment. drake.dlt APPLICATION SURFACE is
rewritten; BRACKETS has two openers.

